---
import { SitePage } from '@boombapbot/editor';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Stay Stoked, Stay Woke · boombapbot"
  description="A footnote to a drum machine made by a white guy in tech, about whose music made drum machines matter."
>
  <SitePage client:load baseUrl={base} current="manifesto" layout="note">
    <h1>Stay Stoked, Stay Woke</h1>
    <p>
      Boom bap* is Black music. It grew out of Black hip-hop communities, and a lot of the records that shaped it tell
      stories about Black life. I’m a white guy who works in tech, and I built a drum machine app called BoomBapBot. I
      took the name from a sound Black artists created and continue to shape. The least I can do is give them credit and
      be honest about what this app owes them. Black artists laid the foundations for much of the music I love, from
      jazz and rock to funk, dub, house and trap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Much of what we love about drum machines came from the passion and ingenuity of Black musicians. Afrika
      Bambaataa, Frankie Knuckles and Juan Atkins helped put drum machines at the heart of electro, house and techno. J
      Dilla’s beats still inspire beatmakers everywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chattel slavery, Jim Crow, redlining and violent attacks on people fighting for desegregation and Black voting
      rights are part of this country’s history. The murder of George Floyd is part of that history, too. I don’t have
      answers for all of that, but I think a good start is just honesty. I’m charging for some features in an app named
      after Black music. If I’m going to do that, I want to be clear about who made the music and why it matters to me.
    </p>
    <p class="sb-aside">
      * Boom bap is a late ’80s and ’90s hip-hop style with punchy kicks and snares, popularized by Pete Rock, KRS-One
      and others.
    </p>
  </SitePage>
</Base>
